# rlschreib.msg - Otis Pruett, Mayor Ascorti's clerk (Redding, the Slag)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

{100}{}{(A thin man in sleeve garters sits on an ore crate with a ledger on his knees.) Otis Pruett, clerk to His Honor the Mayor. You want to open a business in the old ore shed. His Honor sells licenses. The license comes with a deed to the Wanamingo mine. It's a package. Everything in Redding is a package.}
{101}{}{What does the package cost?}
{102}{}{(The clerk glances at a dark stain on the floorboards.) The last clerk had an accident. I'm the new one. His Honor says the price hasn't changed.}
{103}{}{[Speech] Skip the package. Put it on the weekly fee and we both save the paperwork.}
{104}{}{Tell me about Ascorti.}
{105}{}{[Threaten] Tell His Honor the Slag opens without his paper.}
{106}{}{I'll be back.}
{107}{}{License?}

# The package
{110}{}{A thousand dollars. Then seventy-five a week, which His Honor calls a license fee and everybody else calls what it is. The mine deed is thrown in. The mine is full of monsters. That's also a package.}
{111}{}{Here's a thousand.}
{112}{}{[Barter] Six hundred. The mine is worth nothing, and we both know it.}
{113}{}{Let's talk about something else.}
{114}{}{(He writes a receipt with great care and stamps it twice.) Paid in full. His Honor will be pleased. He's always pleased on the day he gets paid.}
{115}{}{(He sighs and crosses out a number.) Six hundred. His Honor will say I'm soft. His Honor says that about everybody who isn't him.}

# The surcharge
{121}{}{(He thinks about it longer than he needs to.) A hundred and twenty-five a week, then. His Honor likes a steady income better than a lump sum. Lumps get spent. Weeks keep coming.}

# Ascorti and the ledger
{130}{}{(He lowers his voice.) His Honor hasn't paid me in three months. He keeps a ledger in the desk at the town hall. Every bribe in Redding, in his own hand. He doesn't trust anybody else to count them.}
{131}{}{[Steal] I'll lift it out of his desk while he's at the Malamute.}
{132}{}{[Lockpick] I'll open his desk tonight.}
{134}{}{[Speech] (Take the ledger to Sheriff Marion.)}
{136}{}{(But a ledger is only paper. Without somebody who can make the sheriff believe it, it's worth nothing.)}
{137}{}{(The town hall is dark after midnight. The ledger is where Pruett said: every name in Redding, every payment, every week, in the mayor's careful hand. Your name isn't in it yet.)}
{138}{}{(Sheriff Marion reads the ledger twice without a word. Then he puts it in his coat.) "The Slag can open. I'll be watching it, and nobody will be collecting from it. Not Ascorti. Not you either, if you ever get ideas." (When you come back, Pruett is packing his things. He's smiling.)}
{139}{}{Let's talk about something else.}

# Threat
{141}{}{(Pruett goes pale and writes something without looking at the paper.) I'll tell His Honor. I'll tell him the Slag is open and that nobody's collecting. I think he'll understand. (Sheriff Marion will hear about it by evening. Sheriff Marion hears about everything.)}

# The license is done
{190}{}{There's a woman waiting outside. Nell Harrow. She ran the laundry in the camp until the Jet took her husband. She's been asking about this shed for weeks. I'd hire her. Nobody else in Redding will.}
{191}{}{Send her in.}

# Look
{300}{}{A thin man in sleeve garters with a ledger on his knees.}
{301}{}{Otis Pruett, clerk to Mayor Ascorti. He sells licenses and hasn't been paid in months.}
